= a
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The point $1+i$ has <modulus> $\sqrt2$ and <argument> $\pi/4$ modulo $2\pi$. Taking complex square roots halves the argument and takes the square root of the modulus:
$$
\boxed{|z|=2^{1/4},\qquad \arg z=\frac\pi8+k\pi\pmod{2\pi},\quad k=0,1.}
$$
The two roots are opposite points in the <complex plane>, explicitly $\pm[\sqrt{(\sqrt2+1)/2}+i\sqrt{(\sqrt2-1)/2}]$.

\Image[paper-2-complex-square-roots.png]
{title=The complex number $1+i$ and its two square roots}
{description=Original Argand diagram. Dashed circles show moduli $\sqrt2$ and $2^{1/4}$; the root rays differ by $\pi$ and each squares to the ray of argument $\pi/4$.}

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Take the imaginary part of <De Moivre's theorem>, $(\cos\theta+i\sin\theta)^5=\cos5\theta+i\sin5\theta$. With $s=\sin\theta$ and $\cos^2\theta=1-s^2$,
$$
\sin5\theta=5(1-s^2)^2s-10(1-s^2)s^3+s^5=\boxed{16s^5-20s^3+5s}.
$$
The required equation cancels the fifth powers and becomes $5s(1-4s^2)=0$. Thus $\sin\theta=0,\pm1/2$, giving all real solutions
$$
\boxed{\theta=n\pi\quad\text{or}\quad\theta=n\pi\pm\frac\pi6,\qquad n\in\mathbb Z.}
$$

= c
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Use the exponential definitions of <complex sine function> and <complex cosine>. Multiplying the equation by $2i$ gives $e^{iz}-e^{-iz}=-2(e^{iz}+e^{-iz})$, hence $3e^{iz}+e^{-iz}=0$. Since exponentials never vanish, this is equivalent to $e^{2iz}=-1/3$. All branches of the <complex logarithm> give
$$
2iz=-\ln3+i(\pi+2\pi n),\qquad\boxed{z=\frac\pi2+n\pi+\frac i2\ln3,\quad n\in\mathbb Z.}
$$
The derivation does not divide by $\cos z$, so it introduces no lost zero-denominator cases.

= 6
{parent=Paper 2}
