= a
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <partial fraction decomposition> is
$$
\frac{3x^2+5x+1}{x(x+1)(x+2)}=\frac1{2x}+\frac1{x+1}+\frac3{2(x+2)}.
$$
Integrating between one and two gives
$$
\boxed{\frac12\ln2+\ln\frac32+\frac32\ln\frac43=\frac12\ln\frac{32}{3}.}
$$
All logarithm arguments are positive on this integration interval.

= b
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The double-angle identity gives $1+\cos2\theta=2\cos^2\theta$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\int_0^{\pi/4}\frac{d\theta}{1+\cos2\theta}=\frac12\int_0^{\pi/4}\sec^2\theta\,d\theta=\frac12[\tan\theta]_0^{\pi/4}=\frac12.}
$$

= c
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Use <integration by substitution> with $\theta=\pi/4-\varphi/2$, so $\sin\varphi=\cos2\theta$ and $d\varphi=-2d\theta$. Reversing the transformed limits gives
$$
\boxed{\int_0^{\pi/2}\frac{d\varphi}{1+\sin\varphi}=2\int_0^{\pi/4}\frac{d\theta}{1+\cos2\theta}=1.}
$$
The original integrand is finite at both endpoints.

= 12
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=12}

Work with $t>0$ and write $u=t^{-1/2}e^{-x^2/(4t)}$. The <chain rule> gives
$$
u_x=-\frac{x}{2t}u,\qquad u_{xx}=\left(\frac{x^2}{4t^2}-\frac1{2t}\right)u,\qquad u_t=\left(\frac{x^2}{4t^2}-\frac1{2t}\right)u.
$$
Thus $u_{xx}=u_t$: it is a constant multiple of the one-dimensional <Gaussian heat kernel>.

For the modified equation, put $f=g(t)u$. Substitution gives $g u_{xx}=g'u+gu_t-gu/t$. The heat-equation terms cancel, and $u>0$ permits division to obtain $\boxed{g'=g/t}$. Solving this <separable differential equation> gives
$$
\boxed{g(t)=Ct,\qquad f(x,t)=C\sqrt t\,e^{-x^2/(4t)},\quad t>0.}
$$
The zero solution is included by $C=0$. This is the <scalar integrating factor for a reaction-diffusion equation> with reaction coefficient $1/t$, because the modified equation is $f_t=f_{xx}+f/t$. Its sign produces growth. Indeed $\int_{\mathbb R}u\,dx=2\sqrt\pi$ is constant, while $\int_{\mathbb R}f\,dx=2C\sqrt\pi\,t$ increases linearly for $C>0$; neither profile is being assigned unit normalization here.

\Image[paper-2-diffusion-and-growth.png]
{title=Diffusive spreading with conserved mass versus the same spreading with amplitude growth}
{description=Original profiles at $t=0.5,1,2$. The left solution is $t^{-1/2}e^{-x^2/(4t)}$; the right is $\sqrt t\,e^{-x^2/(4t)}$ with $C=1$. Their widths both grow as $\sqrt t$, but their spatial integrals are respectively constant and proportional to $t$.}
